essage per entry must be like:\n\n`[time] evaluator evaluator: harness-eval-a.log#eval_artifact.sent_at carries no logger sent_at...` \u2014 must contain `harness-eval-a.log#eval_artifact` and `.sent_at` and `evaluator`.\n\nBut careful: A3b (must stay green) asserts the NO-SWITCH refusal publishes all six evaluator hand_time fields by field name in `[hand-time]` rows only:\n\n```python\n_HAND_ROWS = [ln for ln in outb.splitlines() if ln.strip().startswith(\"[hand-time]\")]\n_MISSING = [f for f in _EXPECT_FIELDS if not any(f in ln for ln in _HAND_ROWS)]\n```\n\nwhere `_EXPECT_FIELDS = [f\"harness-eval-a.log#{c}.{f}\" for c in _EVAL_CALLS for f in (\"sent_at\",\"received_at\")]`.\n\nCurrently no-switch emits `[hand-time] {field} {file}:{line} ({source})` for each entry, where field = `harness-eval-a.log#call.sent_at`. So A3b passes today. Must not change the no-switch path.\n\nSo we keep no-switch behavior exactly. For the switch path, rather than admitting all hand times, we group: those with source `server-header`/`response-created` are refused as `[time]`; other (typed) hand times are admitted and listed.\n\nNow A4: `j14b` built with `job_copy_logged` \u2014 all harness logs stamped (so no server-header entries), plus typed `passes.pass-a.at`. Under switch, hand should contain only typed entries \u2192 admitted. Good.\n\nBut wait: in the logged copy, are there any other typed hand times? `_stamp_logger_times` stamps all calls. Then hand only has `passes.pass-a.at` mark (inputs-no-ledger). A4 asserts `ht14` includes `passes.pass-a.at` with file and source, and allowance present. Fine.\n\nNow let's think about what exact message and kind. Kind `time`. Message must name: the log file (`harness-eval-a.log` appears in `harness-eval-a.log#...`), the evaluator (`evaluator`), and `.sent_at`.\n\nD14's log is `harness-eval.log` per `_job`: `harness-eval.log`. And the field name includes `harness-eval.log#dim1#p1.sent_at`. Good.\n\nBut is the evaluator named in the message? The entry itself doesn't carry the evaluator id. Hmm. We need \"evaluator\" in out for A2. Could include the who. `flag_calls` currently doesn't receive the evaluator id. Let's extend: in `evaluator_history`, after `flag_calls(log_calls, hand)`, we could mark entries with the evaluator? But flag_calls signature is `flag_calls(calls, hand)`.\n\nBetter: instead of changing flag_calls, in build() when refusing, we can produce a message that names the evaluator. But `hand` entries don't record the evaluator or whether they came from a model evaluator vs maker.\n\nOptions:\n1. Add a field to each hand entry indicating its log's owner (e.g. `who`), and whether it came from a model evaluator's harness log.\n2. Do the refusal inside `evaluator_history`, where `who` is known and the calls are at hand.\n\nThe cleanest: in `evaluator_history`, after reading, we know which calls were unstamped (`c.hand_time_entries()` non-empty) and the evaluator id. We can add refusals there when the switch is on? But `evaluator_history` doesn't know `allow_hand`. Hmm \u2014 actually it doesn't need to: the refusal should happen under BOTH? No \u2014 without the switch, the existing `[hand-time]` refusal is what A3a/A3b assert. If we also add a `[time]` refusal unconditionally, the no-switch output would contain both `[hand-time]` and `[time]` rows. A3a asserts `ra == 3` and every call named \u2014 extra rows fine. A3b only reads `[hand-time]` rows \u2014 fine. But the task says \"Do not weaken, rename or reclassify any existing refusal\" and A1 says \"It must be refused, exactly as it is refused without the switch \u2014 same refusal kind\". Hmm, \"same refusal kind\" \u2014 under D13 without switch, what kind? Without switch, the refusal is `[hand-time]`. Under the switch, A1 requires `\"[time]\" in out`. So the refusal kind must be `time` under the switch. \"exactly as it is refused without the switch \u2014 same refusal kind\" is the prompt's phrasing, but the frozen tests are the spec: A1 demands `[time]`. So under the switch: `[time]`.\n\nSo the design: `allow_hand` must reach the decision. The natural place is at the end of `build()` where hand is assembled \u2014 but we need to distinguish server-header/response-created sources, and name the evaluator.\n\nDistinguishing is easy: `e[\"source\"] in (\"server-header\", \"response-created\")`. Naming the evaluator: we can tag entries when they're collected. In `evaluator_history`, we call `flag_calls(log_calls, hand)`. We could after that annotate the newly added entries with the evaluator id. But entries are keyed by field name. Simpler: change `flag_calls` to accept a `who` label and store it in the entry? The entry dicts are put in hand and later written to `x-plumbline.hand_times[]`; adding an extra key there might break schema validation? Let's check the x-plumbline schema \u2014 is it strict about hand_times entries? The record is validated by `kit/plumbline_verify.py` against `schemas/x-plumbline/x-plumbline-v1.schema.json`, which we can't see. Adding a key could break. Safer: don't add keys to entries; instead track separately.\n\nAlternative: build the refusal message from the field name, which already contains the log name and the call, plus a prefix \"evaluator {id}\". We can collect, at the point of refusal, a mapping from log name to owner. But simpler: have `evaluator_history` return the set of unstamped field names with the evaluator who, e.g. a module-level? No.\n\nCleanest approach: change `flag_calls` signature to `flag_calls(calls, hand, who=None)` and record the entries in `hand` as today, but ALSO record a parallel dict `unstamped_by_model_evaluator`... hmm.\n\nActually, maybe simpler: perform the switch-bound refusal inside `evaluator_history` itself, passing `allow_hand` down. That is: `evaluator_history(..., allow_hand=?)`. Then:\n\n```python\nfor c in log_calls:\n    for e in c.hand_time_entries():\n        if e.get(\"source\") in (\"server-header\", \"response-created\"):\n            ref.add(\"time\", f\"{who}: {e['field']} carries no logger stamp (the time came from {e['source']} in {c.log}); a model evaluator's check time ... refused even under --allow-hand-times\")\n```\n\nBut wait: without the switch, this would add `[time]` rows too. A3a and A3b still pass (extra rows). D13 without switch isn't asserted. But would adding a `[time]` row without the switch change `T7b/E3`'s assertion? E3:\n\n```python\nrc, out = emit(FIX, TMP / \"e3.json\", allow_hand=False)\nres((f\"{S} \u00b7 T7b/E3 every evaluator call the logger did not stamp is named (harness-eval-a.log, server time)\",\n     rc == 3 and out.count(\"harness-eval-a.log#\") >= 3 and \".sent_at\" in out\n     and (\"server-header\" in out or \"response-created\" in out), ...))\n```\n\nIf our new `[time]` rows also mention `harness-eval-a.log#` and `.sent_at`, count increases \u2014 still >= 3. Fine.\n\nBut it would be cleaner to emit the `[time]` refusal only when `allow_hand` is true: the no-switch path already refuses with `[hand-time]`, and the `[time]` refusal is specifically the switch-boundary. And \"Do not weaken, rename or reclassify any existing refusal\" \u2014 adding a `[time]` row in the no-switch path could be viewed as noise but not a reclassification. Still, minimal risk: gate on `allow_hand`.\n\nHmm, but `evaluator_history` doesn't get `allow_hand` currently. We can pass it.\n\nAlternatively, do it at the end of build with tagging. Let's think about which produces the exact outputs required.\n\nRequirement for A2: `\"evaluator\" in out` and `\"harness-eval-a.log#\" in out`. Note `harness-eval-a.log#` \u2014 with the `#`. Our field name for hand entries is `f\"{self.log}#{self.name or self.i}.sent_at\"` \u2192 `harness-eval-a.log#eval_artifact.sent_at`. Contains `harness-eval-a.log#`. Good. And A1 requires `\"[time]\" in out` (the ref printing produces `  [time] ...`).\n\nAlso A1 requires exit 3 and nothing written.\n\nD13 requires `\"harness-eval.log\" in out`. Field includes it.\n\nD14 requires `.sent_at` in out \u2014 yes, and each call name `dim1#p1..p3`. Entries exist for each call (three entries), so three refusals. But wait: D14's passes only link `dim1#p1`; but `flag_calls` flags ALL calls in the log. So all three get entries. Good, three `.sent_at` refusals, each naming its call.\n\nBut hold on: in D13/D14, the evaluator's script uses `harness_log.read(log, allow_unstamped=\"skip\")` which returns empty; then `H.read_log` gets 3 calls. Fine.\n\nNow, what about the maker? In these tests the maker kind is... `_job` custody.json has `evaluators: [{id: evaluator, kind: evaluator_kind}]` and no `maker` key at all? Let's re-read `_job`:\n\n```python\ncustody = {\n    \"custody_inputs\": \"plumbline/custody-inputs/v1\",\n    \"orchestrator\": {...},\n    \"bar\": {...},\n    \"evaluators\": [{\"id\": \"evaluator\", ..., \"kind\": evaluator_kind, \"harness_log\": \"custody/evidence/harness-eval.log\", \"model\": {...}, \"runs\": 3}],\n    \"passes\": {...},\n    \"retention\": {...},\n    \"disposition\": {\"state\": \"not_yet_given\", \"by\": \"Steve Moitozo II\"},\n}\n```\n\nNo `maker` key \u2192 `ci.get(\"maker\") or {}` = {}, `m.get(\"kind\")` None \u2192 maker_history not called. Good. So the only hand entries come from the evaluator log.\n\nNote `packet.json` in `_job`:\n```python\n(job / \"packet.json\").write_text(json.dumps(\n    {\"job_id\": \"harness-log-j\", \"terminal_state\": \"accepted\", \"outcome\": \"accepted\",\n     \"disposition_state\": \"not_yet_given\"}))\n```\nNo maker, no buyer. `relay_only_buyer` \u2192 packet.get(\"buyer\") falsy \u2192 False.\n\nNow the `disposition`: `dsp = {\"state\": \"not_yet_given\", \"by\": \"Steve Moitozo II\"}`, no `at`, no dispose ledger line \u2192 `at = H.iso(now)`. Then `mark(\"disposition.at\", ...)`? No: `elif dsp.get(\"at\")` false, and `if not at: at = H.iso(now)` \u2014 no mark. So `disposition.at` is not a hand time. Good.\n\n`bar.approvals` \u2014 bar has no approvals \u2192 gaps.\n\nOK.\n\nNow also: d13 requires the record NOT written. Since ref non-empty \u2192 main returns 3 before writing. Good, as long as our new refusal is added to ref during build.\n\nNow the crucial subtlety: the refusal must be added during `build()` regardless of whether the evaluator is actually linked to a check. In D13/D14, the pass links `dim1#p1`. But also, do we add refusals for hand entries with `server-header` source from the MAKER log? In these tests there is no maker. But in A4's job (logged), no such entries.\n\nBut consider V1/V3/T2 and other must-stay-green cases: `LOGGED` is stamped, so no server-header entries. T2 (no switch) with FIX: server-header entries \u2192 hand-time refusals as before (we don't change no-switch), plus maybe new `[time]` rows if we add unconditionally. Let's avoid adding unconditionally: gate on allow_hand.\n\nBut careful: T3 case (`custody \u00b7 T3 with --allow-hand-times the record is written and hand_times lists...`) is listed as one of the other jobs' reds! \"Five reds in tests/validate_schemas.py belong to other jobs. Do not attempt them and do not make them green: the line-609 [time] refusal case, the 'hand time 59 s off' case, T2c, T3's switch case, and E2.\"\n\nHmm, T3's switch case is red now and stays red (belongs to another job). Let me examine it:\n\n```python\no = TMP / \"t3-allow.json\"\nrc, out = emit(LOGGED, o, allow_hand=True)\nrx = json.loads(o.read_text())[\"predicate\"][\"x-plumbline\"] if o.is_file() else {}\nht = rx.get(\"hand_times\") or []\nby_field = {e.get(\"field\"): e for e in ht}\nres((f\"{S} \u00b7 T3 with --allow-hand-times the record is written and hand_times lists the hand fields with file:line\",\n     rc == 0 and \"disposition.at\" in by_field and by_field[\"disposition.at\"].get(\"line\") is not None\n     and len(ht) >= 1 and all(e.get(\"file\") and e.get(\"source\") for e in ht), f\"exit {rc}: {out.strip()[-140:]}\"))\n```\n\nIt's currently red (presumably because `disposition.at` line is None or something). Our change shouldn't make it green or more red \u2014 we just must not \"attempt\" it. LOGGED is stamped, so our new refusal won't fire. Good.\n\nNow A4: `job_copy_logged` \u2014 all logs stamped, typed `passes.pass-a.at`. Under switch, hand contains `passes.pass-a.at` with source `inputs-no-ledger`. Our gate: only `server-header`/`response-created` sources refused \u2192 A4 writes. Good.\n\nNow, where exactly to implement? Let me design a clean minimal change.\n\nOption A: in `build()`, at the hand-time assembly point:\n\n```python\nxp[\"hand_times\"] = [hand[k] for k in hand]\nif hand and allow_hand:\n    xp[\"allowances\"] = {...}\nif hand and not allow_hand:\n    for e in hand.values():\n        ref.add(\"hand-time\", ...)\n```\n\nChange to:\n\n```python\n# The switch is about HAND-ENTERED times. A time the reader synthesized from a harness log call's server\n# `Date` header or `response created` epoch is NOT hand-entered: the logger stamped nothing, so the model\n# evaluator's check time has no logger source at all. That is refused under --allow-hand-times too, as a\n# `time` refusal naming the log and every unstamped call (a model evaluator with an unsourced check time),\n# exactly as the no-switch path refuses a model evaluator with no harness log.\nunsourced = [e for e in hand.values() if e.get(\"source\") in (\"server-header\", \"response-created\")]\nif unsourced:\n    for e in unsourced:\n        ref.add(\"time\", f\"{e['field']}: {e['source']} in {e['file']} ...\")\n```\n\nWait, but the message must contain \"evaluator\". Hmm.\n\n`f\"{e['field']} ...\"` doesn't contain \"evaluator\". We need to name the evaluator. Where do we know that? We can tag each hand entry with the owner when collected, but avoid putting the tag into the emitted JSON.\n\nApproach: change `flag_calls(calls, hand, who=None)` to also record into a companion dict, e.g. a module-level? No globals.\n\nBetter: have `flag_calls` return the list of entries it created, and let callers record ownership separately. Then `hand` remains entries only; a parallel dict `hand_owner: dict[str, str]` maps field \u2192 who. In `maker_history` and `evaluator_history`, we can pass a `owners` dict.\n\nActually, simplest robust approach: put the refusal in `evaluator_history` with `allow_hand` passed down. It has `who` = `f\"evaluator {e.get('id')}\"` \u2014 contains \"evaluator\". \n\nBut also, maker calls could be unstamped in a switch run \u2014 is that in scope? The prompt: \"a time that exists only because the evaluator's harness-log call carried no logger sent_at/received_at\". The bar only covers the evaluator. For the maker, `maker_history` unstamped calls \u2192 hand entries with server-header. Under switch, currently admitted. Should we refuse? The prompt focuses on the evaluator. The tests don't cover maker under switch. Changing the maker too might break... hmm, would it break any green? Let's check which tests use maker with unstamped logs and `--allow-hand-times` and expect rc 0.\n\nLook at `fail-*` fixtures: `job_copy(..., overlay)` uses LOGGED (stamped) then overlays failures. Then `refused(...)` uses `emit(j, o, **kw)` with default `allow_hand=True` in `emit()` signature:\n\n```python\ndef emit(job, out, tree=None, extra=(), allow_hand=True):\n```\n\nSo failure fixtures are emitted with `--allow-hand-times` by default! And they expect exit 3 with a tag like \"time\", \"endpoint\", \"order\", \"approval\". If overlays add unstamped calls, our new refusal might fire and change the first reported kind? No \u2014 the assertion is `f\"[{tag}]\" in out and not o.exists()`, so extra refusals of other kinds don't break it. But if our new refusal causes a refusal where the case expects success... Let's check cases expecting success with allow_hand=True.\n\nSuccess cases with `emit(..., allow_hand=True)` and rc==0:\n- `emit(j, TMP / \"given.json\")` \u2014 job_copy(\"given\") = LOGGED + no overlay. Stamped. OK.\n- `emit(j, TMP / \"nodisp.json\")` \u2014 LOGGED. OK.\n- `emit(j, TMP / \"remedy.json\")` \u2014 LOGGED.\n- `emit(j, TMP / \"nodim.json\")`.\n- `emit(j, TMP / \"same.json\")` \u2014 job_copy same-family, rewrites maker log replacing text \u2014 still stamped.\n- `emit(j, TMP / \"noinputs.json\")` \u2014 custody.json deleted; no evaluator/maker facts, so no hand from logs (nothing reads them). OK.\n- `emit(j, TMP / \"open.json\")` \u2014 exits 2 before build.\n- `T3` (logged) \u2014 red anyway.\n- `emit(j, TMP / \"tol59.json\")` \u2014 `job_copy_logged(\"tol-59\")` \u2014 all logs stamped after constructing? `job_copy_logged` stamps. OK.\n- pin tests: `emit(j, TMP / \"unpinned.json\")` uses `job_copy(\"pin-unpinned\", \"routing-unpinned\")` \u2014 LOGGED + overlay. The overlay may add logs? `routing-unpinned` overlay \u2014 unknown but likely content only. If it contains unstamped harness logs and expects rc == 1 with a hard gap... Then our new refusal would make rc 3 and the case red. Hmm, risky.\n\nLet me look: `res((f\"{S} \u00b7 a request without no-fallback + ZDR is written with a HARD gap (exit 1), ...\", rc == 1 and ...))`. This uses job_copy with overlay \"routing-unpinned\". Since `job_copy` copies LOGGED (stamped), and the overlay adds/replaces files. If the overlay replaces the maker harness log with an unstamped one, then under allow_hand=True our new refusal (if applied to maker) would fire, exit 3, breaking it. If we only apply to evaluator, then the overlay's evaluator log (if it replaces) might also be unstamped. Hmm.\n\nWe don't have the overlay contents. The prompt says these cases must stay green? It lists \"V1/V3/T2, T3, E2\" and A3a/A3b/A4/T7b/E3 and the other 13 tests in test_evaluator_harness_log.py. The routing cases aren't explicitly listed, but \"If a case you were not asked to change goes red, fix your change rather than the test.\" So all other passing cases must remain.\n\nSafer approach: gate the new refusal so it fires only for hand entries or