n the record whose only source is a hand is a HAND TIME: a ledger line (inputs/\ncustody-events.jsonl) with `writer: \"hand\"` or with no `writer` at all, a time typed into inputs/custody.json with\nno ledger line behind it, or a server-set time from a harness log call with no logger `sent_at`/`received_at` (the\nresponse `Date` header or its `created` epoch). The emitter REFUSES hand times by default: exit 3, kind\n`hand-time`, every field named with its file:line, nothing written. `--allow-hand-times` writes the record, lists\neach hand time in `x-plumbline.hand_times[]` and records the allowance in `x-plumbline.allowances.hand_times`. The\nswitch FLAGS hand times; it never verifies them, and it silences nothing else.\n```\n\nReplace with same + new paragraph. I'll append:\n\n```\nswitch FLAGS hand times; it never verifies them, and it silences nothing else.\n\nThe switch is about times TYPED into the custody inputs, never an unsourced one. A model evaluator's check time\nthat exists only because its harness-log call carried no logger `sent_at`/`received_at`, so the reader fell back\nto a server `Date` header or a response `created` epoch, has no logger source at all; `--allow-hand-times` does\nnot admit it. It is refused (exit 3, kind `time`, naming the log and EVERY unstamped call) exactly as a model\nevaluator with no harness log is refused.\n```\n\nGood.\n\nNow build the blocks."}, "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze the problem carefully.\n\nThe failing tests:\n- `J14/A1`: a model evaluator's check time is refused UNDER `--allow-hand-times`, naming the log (`harness-eval-a.log`), exit 3, `[time]`, `harness-eval-a.log` in output, nothing written.\n- `J14/A2`: the refusal cites the EVALUATOR path, not only the maker's (`evaluator` in out, `harness-eval-a.log#` in out), exit 3.\n- `test_d13`: under `--allow-hand-times` an unsourced model-evaluator check time is still refused, naming the log (`harness-eval.log`), and no record written.\n- `test_d14`: the naming E3 delivered survives the switch \u2014 every call named (`dim1#p1`, `dim1#p2`, `dim1#p3`), and `.sent_at` named.\n\nSo the refusal kind must be `[time]` (A1 asserts `\"[time]\" in out`; A2 asserts `\"evaluator\" in out` and `\"harness-eval-a.log#\" in out`).\n\nCurrently what happens under `--allow-hand-times`? `hand_times` are collected; with `allow_hand` the emitter writes `allowances` and does NOT refuse per hand-time entry. But `flag_calls` collects the unstamped call entries into `hand`, so under `--allow-hand-times` no refusal is added at all for those entries \u2014 the record is written. That's the bug.\n\nSo we need: under `--allow-hand-times`, hand-time entries whose `source` is `server-header` or `response-created` (i.e. synthesised by the reader from the log) must still be refused \u2014 but with kind `[time]`, naming the log (`harness-eval-a.log`) and every unstamped call (`.sent_at` field name, `harness-eval-a.log#call`).\n\nWait, but also this must not break A4 (genuine hand-entered time admitted) and A3a/A3b (no-switch refusal names every call and publishes six fields by field name).\n\nLet me look at the test d13/d14 more carefully:\n\n```python\nUNSTAMPED = {\"call\": \"dim1#p1\",\n             \"request\": {\"url\": \"https://api.z.ai/chat/completions\"},\n             \"response\": {\"model\": \"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash\"},\n             \"response_headers\": {\"Date\": \"Sun, 08 Nov 2026 10:00:09 GMT\"}}\n\ndef test_d13_switch_does_not_admit_an_unsourced_evaluator_time(self):\n    \"\"\"D13: under --allow-hand-times an unsourced model-evaluator check time is still refused, naming the log.\"\"\"\n    job = self._job(json.dumps(self.UNSTAMPED) + \"\\n\")\n    r = self._emit(job, allow_hand_times=True)\n    out = r.stdout + r.stderr\n    self.assertNotEqual(r.returncode, 0,\n                        f\"the switch admitted an unsourced model-evaluator time\\n{out}\")\n    self.assertIn(\"harness-eval.log\", out, f\"the refusal does not name the log\\n{out}\")\n    self.assertIsNone(self._record(job), f\"a refused record was written anyway\\n{out}\")\n\ndef test_d14_switch_refusal_names_every_unstamped_call(self):\n    \"\"\"D14: the naming E3 delivered survives the switch -- every call named, not aborted at the first.\"\"\"\n    log = [self.UNSTAMPED,\n           dict(self.UNSTAMPED, call=\"dim1#p2\"),\n           dict(self.UNSTAMPED, call=\"dim1#p3\")]\n    job = self._job(json.dumps(log) + \"\\n\")\n    r = self._emit(job, allow_hand_times=True)\n    out = r.stdout + r.stderr\n    self.assertNotEqual(r.returncode, 0, f\"an unsourced evaluator time was admitted\\n{out}\")\n    for n in (1, 2, 3):\n        self.assertIn(f\"dim1#p{n}\", out, f\"call dim1#p{n} was not named\\n{out}\")\n    self.assertIn(\".sent_at\", out, f\"the unstamped field is not named\\n{out}\")\n```\n\nNote: this test's log has 3 calls, none stamped. But the custody.json in `_job` has one pass `dim1#p1`. So evaluator_history reads the log \u2014 `harness_log.read(log, allow_unstamped=\"skip\")` returns empty dict (all unstamped, skipped). Then `H.read_log` returns 3 calls; `flag_calls(log_calls, hand)` puts entries for all 3 calls with `.sent_at`/`.received_at` sources from server-header.\n\nThen `calls = [c for c in log_calls if c.at]` \u2014 c.at is derived from Date header, so all 3 present. `annotate` \u2014 model matching: `z-ai/glm-5.3-flash` matches. `check_pin_order(pin=None)` no-op.\n\nThen named = each call name.\n\nThen in build: for the pass, `call = link_call(...)` with `f.get(\"call\")` = \"dim1#p1\"? Actually in the test `_job`, custody.json `passes` has `{\"dim1#p1\": {\"evaluator\": \"evaluator\", \"call\": \"dim1#p1\", \"run\": 1, ...}}`. Hmm actually passes dict key is \"dim1#p1\". `pfacts.get(pid)` \u2014 pid comes from `passes_src`. Since there's no \u00a75 record, `passes_src = [{\"pass_id\": pid} for pid in (ci.get(\"passes\") or {})]` \u2192 pass_id \"dim1#p1\". f = pfacts[\"dim1#p1\"] = the dict with call \"dim1#p1\".\n\n`linkable` = True since f.get(\"call\") in ev_map (named maps \"dim1#p1\" and also \"dim1#p2\", \"dim1#p3\"). So call = the call. Then cross_check(ref, ..., f.get(\"at\") which is None) no-op. at_v = H.iso(call.at) \u2014 server Date. xc at_basis = \"response Date header\". `call.source_at()` returns `harness-eval.log:N` for the Date header line. So xc[\"at_source\"] = that.\n\nHmm, but the log in D13/D14 is written as JSON line `json.dumps(self.UNSTAMPED) + \"\\n\"` \u2014 one line. So `_line_of` returns line 1.\n\nThen `entry = (ev_entries.get(...) or {}).get(f.get(\"call\"))` \u2014 entries is empty (skip). So entry None.\n\nThen checks built with at_v from the server Date.\n\nThen at end of build: `xp[\"hand_times\"] = [hand[k] for k in hand]`; `if hand and allow_hand: xp[\"allowances\"] = ...`; `if hand and not allow_hand: for e in hand.values(): ref.add(\"hand-time\", ...)`.\n\nUnder allow_hand=True, no refusal \u21d2 record written. This is the bug.\n\nNow what must change: under allow_hand, entries whose source is `server-header` or `response-created` must be refused with kind `time` (not hand-time), naming the log and every call.\n\nWait \u2014 A1 asserts `\"[time]\" in out`, and `harness-eval-a.log` in out, exit 3. A2 asserts `\"evaluator\" in out` and `\"harness-eval-a.log#\" in out`.\n\nD14 asserts `.sent_at` in out and each `dim1#pN` in out. It does not check kind.\n\nSo the refusal message per entry must be like:\n\n`[time] evaluator evaluator: harness-eval-a.log#eval_artifact.sent_at carries no logger sent_at...` \u2014 must contain `harness-eval-a.log#eval_artifact` and `.sent_at` and `evaluator`.\n\nBut careful: A3b (must stay green) asserts the NO-SWITCH refusal publishes all six evaluator hand_time fields by field name in `[hand-time]` rows only:\n\n```python\n_HAND_ROWS = [ln for ln in outb.splitlines() if ln.strip().startswith(\"[hand-time]\")]\n_MISSING = [f for f in _EXPECT_FIELDS if not any(f in ln for ln in _HAND_ROWS)]\n```\n\nwhere `_EXPECT_FIELDS = [f\"harness-eval-a.log#{c}.{f}\" for c in _EVAL_CALLS for f in (\"sent_at\",\"received_at\")]`.\n\nCurrently no-switch emits `[hand-time] {field} {file}:{line} ({source})` for each entry, where field = `harness-eval-a.log#call.sent_at`. So A3b passes today. Must not change the no-switch path.\n\nSo we keep no-switch behavior exactly. For the switch path, rather than admitting all hand times, we group: those with source `server-header`/`response-created` are refused as `[time]`; other (typed) hand times are admitted and listed.\n\nNow A4: `j14b` built with `job_copy_logged` \u2014 all harness logs stamped (so no server-header entries), plus typed `passes.pass-a.at`. Under switch, hand should contain only typed entries \u2192 admitted. Good.\n\nBut wait: in the logged copy, are there any other typed hand times? `_stamp_logger_times` stamps all calls. Then hand only has `passes.pass-a.at` mark (inputs-no-ledger). A4 asserts `ht14` includes `passes.pass-a.at` with file and source, and allowance present. Fine.\n\nNow let's think about what exact message and kind. Kind `time`. Message must name: the log file (`harness-eval-a.log` appears in `harness-eval-a.log#...`), the evaluator (`evaluator`), and `.sent_at`.\n\nD14's log is `harness-eval.log` per `_job`: `harness-eval.log`. And the field name includes `harness-eval.log#dim1#p1.sent_at`. Good.\n\nBut is the evaluator named in the message? The entry itself doesn't carry the evaluator id. Hmm. We need \"evaluator\" in out for A2. Could include the who. `flag_calls` currently doesn't receive the evaluator id. Let's extend: in `evaluator_history`, after `flag_calls(log_calls, hand)`, we could mark entries with the evaluator? But flag_calls signature is `flag_calls(calls, hand)`.\n\nBetter: instead of changing flag_calls, in build() when refusing, we can produce a message that names the evaluator. But `hand` entries don't record the evaluator or whether they came from a model evaluator vs maker.\n\nOptions:\n1. Add a field to each hand entry indicating its log's owner (e.g. `who`), and whether it came from a model evaluator's harness log.\n2. Do the refusal inside `evaluator_history`, where `who` is known and the calls are at hand.\n\nThe cleanest: in `evaluator_history`, after reading, we know which calls were unstamped (`c.hand_time_entries()` non-empty) and the evaluator id. We can add refusals there when the switch is on? But `evaluator_history` doesn't know `allow_hand`. Hmm \u2014 actually it doesn't need to: the refusal should happen under BOTH? No \u2014 without the switch, the existing `[hand-time]` refusal is what A3a/A3b assert. If we also add a `[time]` refusal unconditionally, the no-switch output would contain both `[hand-time]` and `[time]` rows. A3a asserts `ra == 3` and every call named \u2014 extra rows fine. A3b only reads `[hand-time]` rows \u2014 fine. But the task says \"Do not weaken, rename or reclassify any existing refusal\" and A1 says \"It must be refused, exactly as it is refused without the switch \u2014 same refusal kind\". Hmm, \"same refusal kind\" \u2014 under D13 without switch, what kind? Without switch, the refusal is `[hand-time]`. Under the switch, A1 requires `\"[time]\" in out`. So the refusal kind must be `time` under the switch. \"exactly as it is refused without the switch \u2014 same refusal kind\" is the prompt's phrasing, but the frozen tests are the spec: A1 demands `[time]`. So under the switch: `[time]`.\n\nSo the design: `allow_hand` must reach the decision. The natural place is at the end of `build()` where hand is assembled \u2014 but we need to distinguish server-header/response-created sources, and name the evaluator.\n\nDistinguishing is easy: `e[\"source\"] in (\"server-header\", \"response-created\")`. Naming the evaluator: we can tag entries when they're collected. In `evaluator_history`, we call `flag_calls(log_calls, hand)`. We could after that annotate the newly added entries with the evaluator id. But entries are keyed by field name. Simpler: change `flag_calls` to accept a `who` label and store it in the entry? The entry dicts are put in hand and later written to `x-plumbline.hand_times[]`; adding an extra key there might break schema validation? Let's check the x-plumbline schema \u2014 is it strict about hand_times entries? The record is validated by `kit/plumbline_verify.py` against `schemas/x-plumbline/x-plumbline-v1.schema.json`, which we can't see. Adding a key could break. Safer: don't add keys to entries; instead track separately.\n\nAlternative: build the refusal message from the field name, which already contains the log name and the call, plus a prefix \"evaluator {id}\". We can collect, at the point of refusal, a mapping from log name to owner. But simpler: have `evaluator_history` return the set of unstamped field names with the evaluator who, e.g. a module-level? No.\n\nCleanest approach: change `flag_calls` signature to `flag_calls(calls, hand, who=None)` and record the entries in `hand` as today, but ALSO record a parallel dict `unstamped_by_model_evaluator`... hmm.\n\nActually, maybe simpler: perform the switch-bound refusal inside `evaluator_history` itself, passing `allow_hand` down. That is: `evaluator_history(..., allow_hand=?)`. Then:\n\n```python\nfor c in log_calls:\n    for e in c.hand_time_entries():\n        if e.get(\"source\") in (\"server-header\", \"response-created\"):\n            ref.add(\"time\", f\"{who}: {e['field']} carries no logger stamp (the time came from {e['source']} in {c.log}); a model evaluator's check time ... refused even under --allow-hand-times\")\n```\n\nBut wait: without the switch, this would add `[time]` rows too. A3a and A3b still pass (extra rows). D13 without switch isn't asserted. But would adding a `[time]` row without the switch change `T7b/E3`'s assertion? E3:\n\n```python\nrc, out = emit(FIX, TMP / \"e3.json\", allow_hand=False)\nres((f\"{S} \u00b7 T7b/E3 every evaluator call the logger did not stamp is named