xture likely has `at` in custody.json. Marked as `inputs-no-ledger` \u2192 hand entry.\n\nAlso `link_call`: `f.get(\"call\")` \u2014 base fixture passes has `\"call\": \"eval_artifact\"` presumably. `ev_calls` from evaluator_history `calls` \u2014 calls present. So `call` linked; `cross_check` on typed at vs call.at (which is server Date) \u2014 if differs > 60s, refusal `time`. Maybe that's why the fixture is refused currently with `[time]`? Hmm, if so the case would already be green. But the prompt says it's red.\n\nLet me actually think about `evaluator_history`: `entries = harness_log.read(log, allow_unstamped=\"skip\")`. For FIX's evaluator log with entries lacking sent_at/received_at \u2192 skipped \u2192 entries = {}. Then `ev_entries[id] = {}`. In build's check loop, `entry = (ev_entries.get(...) or {}).get(f.get(\"call\"))` \u2192 None. So no log-sourced time; typed `at` used. And `link_call` gives the call with `.at` = Date header. Cross-check typed at against it.\n\nLet's look at fixture contents. We don't have the fixture files. Hmm. `tests/fixtures/custody/job-rejected/`. Not provided. But from validate_schemas usage: \"the fixture's logs carry server times only, so a MODEL evaluator's check times have no logged source and the emitter REFUSES the record (D6/D7)\". And D6 in test_evaluator_harness_log is about the `_job` local fixture, not this one.\n\nHmm, the case at line ~609 (in current file) expects `[time]` refusal. The tests/test_evaluator_harness_log D6 says a MODEL evaluator with NO harness log refuses; the emitter's evaluator_history adds `time` refusal when log is None. In FIX, the log exists but has no logger times. With `allow_unstamped=\"skip\"`, entries={}; but maybe `link_call` then... Hmm the typed pass `at` in the fixture: if the call's date differs, `cross_check` adds `time`. Or maybe not.\n\nActually, maybe the case is red because the emitter currently WRITES a record under the switch (exit 0), so `rc == 3` fails. That is plausible. And the prompt says don't make it green... That's contradictory. Unless the prompt's list means: these reds are caused by other, pre-existing defects, and \"do not attempt them\" = don't try to fix causes outside your change; \"do not make them green\" maybe meaning don't take actions (like extra refusals unrelated) to green them?? Hmm, but if our correct change naturally greens one, that would be a conflict. Let me re-read the prompt's exact wording:\n\n\"Five reds in `tests/validate_schemas.py` belong to **other jobs**. Do not attempt them and do not make them green: the line-609 `[time]` refusal case, the \"hand time 59 s off\" case, `T2c`, `T3`'s switch case, and `E2`.\"\n\nHmm \u2014 perhaps these are reds because of a DIFFERENT bug (e.g., the fixture's harness logs get rewritten by `_stamp_logger_times`? no). Or maybe the \"line-609 `[time]` refusal case\" is red for a reason orthogonal to our change: e.g., the required `[time]` message needs to name the harness log, and currently the refusal under the switch is absent; after our change it would pass. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe I mis-identify which test is \"line-609 `[time]` refusal case\". The file given is long; line 609 might be something else, like a case expecting `[time]` about a pass linked to no call. We can't rely on line numbers.\n\nLet's consider: maybe those five reds are red for reasons like the tests expect `--allow-hand-times` to admit something but the current code refuses, etc. The instruction is primarily: don't widen the change; if a case you weren't asked to change goes red, fix your change. The phrase \"do not make them green\" might be about not adding hacks to satisfy them.\n\nI think the right approach: implement exactly the specified behaviour, confined narrowly to *model evaluator* server-derived times. That's what the bar asks. Then report tally and reds. I cannot run the tests, so I must reason.\n\nNow, which sources to refuse unconditionally? `hand_time_entries()` gives `source` in {\"ser